# Where a file came from (#229).
#
# A file records where it was obtained from (aprov:sourceLocation), so that a drawing can always be traced to its origin:
# a public archive's address for a public drawing, an organisation's own storage when that is the only origin known.
# The ontology cannot require it: under the open-world assumption a minimum cardinality would infer an origin nobody
# recorded (#84). So the rule is a closed-world check on DATA. It serves FR 9 of the UC-01 ORSD (a drawing can be
# traced to its source) and competency question CQ 9.5.
#
# A file made from another file (a format conversion, a page split out of a PDF) has other bytes, so it is another
# DrawingFile. It records its origin through prov:wasDerivedFrom to the file it was made from, and only the original
# records a sourceLocation: the path below follows that chain, so a derived file whose original has a source conforms.
#
# Severity: sh:Warning. A file with no recorded origin is incomplete, not malformed: the origin may be unknown.

@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix aprov: <https://w3id.org/adiro/adiro_provenance#> .
@prefix dv: <https://w3id.org/adiro/data-validation/file-source#> .

dv:FileSource a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass aprov:DrawingFile ;
    sh:severity sh:Warning ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:alternativePath ( aprov:sourceLocation ( [ sh:oneOrMorePath prov:wasDerivedFrom ] aprov:sourceLocation ) ) ] ;
        sh:severity sh:Warning ;  # a property shape does not inherit the severity of its node shape
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:message "A file records where it was obtained from (sourceLocation), or was derived from a file that does."
    ] .
